<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Shakya republic</h1><p class="st-meta">650 BCE – 470 BCE (approx.) · capital Kapilavastu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The small clan republic at the foot of the Himalaya into which Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha, was born.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Shakyas were a clan claiming descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikshvaku">Ikshvaku</a> kings, settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terai">Terai</a> on the border of modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Nepal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. By the 7th or 6th century BCE they governed themselves from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapilavastu">Kapilavastu</a>, now identified either with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tilaurakot">Tilaurakot</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> or with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piprahwa">Piprahwa</a> in India.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Shakyas were ruled by an assembly of clan heads that met in a council hall, with an elected chief; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Buddha</a>'s father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suddhodana">Suddhodana</a> was one such leader, later remembered as a king. They farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and were proud of their lineage. Politically they were dependants of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kosala">Kingdom of Kosala</a>. A relic casket found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piprahwa">Piprahwa</a> carries an inscription naming the Shakyas.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Shortly before or after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Buddha</a>'s death the Kosalan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidudabha">Vidudabha</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapilavastu">Kapilavastu</a> and massacred many Shakyas, ending the republic as an independent state. Their territory became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kosala">Kosala</a> and then of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Magadha</a>, and the Buddha is still called Shakyamuni, sage of the Shakyas.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 650 BCE – 470 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:46.77%;width:3.58%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>650 BCE – 470 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kapilavastu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Suddhodana</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Vedic religion, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>, <a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Shakyas, Sakya Gana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-560&amp;focus=shakya-republic" aria-label="Shakya republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6861 1167 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Shakya republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7181 1423L7155 1425"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7155 1425h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7181 1423h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7181 1423h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kapilavastu. <a href="/atlas?year=-560&amp;focus=shakya-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

